Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns, their usage & likely conditions  
• "refresh_date": locate the most‑recent data snapshot (MAX("refresh_date")).  
• "week": pick the latest week for that snapshot, then calculate the same week one year earlier (DATEADD(year,‑1,…)).  
• "rank": find the top‑ranked record (smallest value; usually 1 or MIN("rank")).  
• "term": the rising search term we ultimately want to display.  
• "percent_gain": optional for extra context when viewing results.  
• "dma_name"/"dma_id": optional when you want to see which U.S. DMA the search term belongs to.  

We will work inside the GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS table.

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 rows each, all identifiers in double quotes) that progress from simple inspection to isolating the single record required by the task.

```sql
--1. Description: List distinct refresh dates to see the data‑update cadence
SELECT DISTINCT "refresh_date"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--2. Description: Return the most recent refresh date available
SELECT MAX("refresh_date") AS "max_refresh_date"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--3. Description: Show weeks that belong to the most‑recent refresh date
SELECT DISTINCT "week"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
ORDER BY "week" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--4. Description: Preview rising terms for the latest refresh date
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
ORDER BY "week" DESC, "rank" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--5. Description: Capture the latest week tied to that refresh date
SELECT MAX("week") AS "latest_week"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--6. Description: Compute the target week (exactly one year earlier than the latest week)
SELECT DATEADD(year, -1,
              MAX("week")) AS "target_week"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--7. Description: List all rising terms for the calculated target week
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
  AND "week" = (SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, MAX("week"))
                FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
                WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS))
ORDER BY "rank" ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--8. Description: Pull only the record(s) ranked #1 during that target week
SELECT "week", "term", "rank", "percent_gain", "dma_name"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
  AND "week" = (SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, MAX("week"))
                FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
                WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS))
  AND "rank" = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--9. Description: Dynamically join on the minimum rank to be safe against non‑1 top ranks
SELECT t."week",
       t."term",
       t."rank",
       t."percent_gain",
       t."dma_name"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS t
JOIN (
      SELECT MIN("rank") AS "min_rank"
      FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
      WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                               FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
        AND "week" = (SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, MAX("week"))
                      FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
                      WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                                               FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS))
     ) r
ON t."rank" = r."min_rank"
WHERE t."refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                           FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
  AND t."week"        = (SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, MAX("week"))
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
                         WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                                                  FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS))
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10. Description: Show the distinct term(s) that ended up as the top rising query one year before the latest week
SELECT DISTINCT "term"
FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS)
  AND "week" = (SELECT DATEADD(year, -1, MAX("week"))
                FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS
                WHERE "refresh_date" = (SELECT MAX("refresh_date")
                                         FROM GOOGLE_TRENDS.GOOGLE_TRENDS.TOP_RISING_TERMS))
  AND "rank" = 1
ORDER BY "term"
LIMIT 20;
```
